WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:00.750
Harvest-now,

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:07.166
decrypt-later risk exists because some encrypted data may remain valuable long after it is captured.

00:00:07.166 --> 00:00:13.154
The attacker in this model may not be able to decrypt the data today.

00:00:13.154 --> 00:00:17.004
The concern is future exposure if cryptographic assumptions change,

00:00:17.004 --> 00:00:18.715
implementation weaknesses become exploitable,

00:00:18.715 --> 00:00:23.420
or advances such as cryptographically relevant quantum computing affect public-key protections.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:28.980
The highest concern is long-lived sensitive data, not every byte of encrypted traffic.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:33.685
The strongest preparation starts with data classification and data flow mapping.

00:00:33.685 --> 00:00:39.673
Teams need to know what information must remain confidential, how long it remains sensitive,

00:00:39.673 --> 00:00:42.667
where it moves, where it is stored,

00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:46.089
and which systems or third parties handle it.

00:00:46.089 --> 00:00:51.222
Defenders should think beyond servers to include TLS endpoints, VPNs, APIs, email,

00:00:51.222 --> 00:00:55.072
file transfers, backups, replication, service-to-service traffic, mobile applications, logs,

00:00:55.072 --> 00:00:56.783
archives, and vendor-managed paths.

00:00:56.783 --> 00:01:02.343
Cryptographic inventory and dependency mapping connect the data risk to real engineering work.

00:01:02.343 --> 00:01:05.765
The inventory should identify algorithms, protocols, certificates, libraries,

00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:10.042
key management systems, hardware security modules, cloud services, third-party products,

00:01:10.042 --> 00:01:13.036
owners, data protected, lifetime, and migration status.

00:01:13.036 --> 00:01:17.741
From there, teams can prioritize based on sensitivity, lifetime, exposure, volume,

00:01:17.741 --> 00:01:20.735
mission importance, vendor readiness, and migration difficulty.

00:01:20.735 --> 00:01:22.874
The goal is not fear.

00:01:22.874 --> 00:01:28.007
The goal is reducing long-term confidentiality risk before emergency migration becomes necessary.

00:01:28.007 --> 00:01:32.712
Strong programs build crypto agility, use standards-based migration paths, test changes,

00:01:32.712 --> 00:01:36.134
coordinate with vendors, reduce unnecessary retention, document assumptions,

00:01:36.134 --> 00:01:38.272
and communicate clearly with leadership.

00:01:38.272 --> 00:01:41.694
Harvest-now, decrypt-later planning is ultimately a resilience exercise:

00:01:41.694 --> 00:01:47.460
know what must stay confidential, know what protects it, and prepare to adapt safely.
